{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01D3RGNm88G7YGVzvwLgm64M","type":"tool_result","content":"18\n25","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ac707f6-2521-40ae-9869-c072add1ef61","uuid":"b4624115-aef9-4ed5-885f-6b51d5184f78","timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:49:03.174Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"18\n25","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_nrsy5oeduhqsbrabpwlkeo7td4pq226souhdoeu6dhpo63lnut7a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. **One real bug found and fixed.**\n\n## The bug\n\n`translation/src/lib.rs` did `let img = &mut *img;` to read the struct fields. Forming a Rust reference asserts non-null/aligned/unique validity, so in **debug** builds a `NULL` `cp_image_t*` aborted with **SIGABRT (6)** *before any load*, while the C traps with **SIGSEGV (11)** from the `img->w` load itself. Fixed by reading through raw pointers (`ptr::addr_of!(…).read()`), and by switching the pixel walk from `offset`/`add` to `wrapping_offset`/`wrapping_add` — C's `data[i]` is plain pointer arithmetic with no single-allocation precondition, and this library is routinely handed `w`/`h` describing more pixels than the buffer holds. Only the Rust was changed; `c_src/` is untouched.\n\n## Phase results\n\n| Phase | Result |\n|---|---|\n| A | `SYMBOLS.md` (81 L), `ERRORS.md` (17 rows), `CONFIGS.md` (24 rows) |\n| B | 24/24 `CONFIGS.md` rows pass, randomized w/ fixed seeds |\n| C | 17/17 `ERRORS.md` rows + generic boundary sweep pass |\n| D | Symbol diff **empty**; all combos/profiles pass |\n\nBoth `.so`s are loaded via `libloading` — the Rust is never called directly, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper and C struct layout are part of what's tested.\n\nNotable coverage: exhaustive 256×256 (alpha × channel) rounding table; guard-pixel padding to catch off-by-one bounds; unaligned `pix` (legal, align 1); `w<0 && h<0` where the double sign flip makes the bound *positive* and the C really does work; wrapping `stride = w*4` / `bound = stride*h` overflow to zero, negative, and small-positive; `fork()`-based signal-parity for the UB rows (`img == NULL`, `pix == NULL` with positive bound). For the small-positive-wrap row, rejection sampling over `i32²` hits it with probability ~2⁻¹⁹, so those `(w,h)` pairs are constructed algebraically via a modular inverse mod 2³².\n\n## Caveats\n\n- **No driver binary** — `c_src/CMakeLists.txt` has no `add_executable` and the crate is `cdylib`-only, so the stdout-comparison gate is N/A (asserted mechanically in `verify.sh`).\n- **No `[features]` table** in `Cargo.toml`, so `default` and `--no-default-features` are the same unit. To keep the \"every configuration\" gate meaningful I substituted axes that *do* change codegen: **debug × release** Rust profiles (this is what exposed the bug — release masked it) × **stock/-O2/-O3** C builds, 12 runs total.\n- Rows 12–14 are UB in both languages; parity there is asserted at the process-signal level, which is the strongest available guarantee, not a language-level one.\n- `cargo clippy` leaves cosmetic lints (doc indentation, `mem::drop` of non-`Drop`); left alone as they don't affect behavior.\n\nRe-run everything with `cd translation && bash verify.sh`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4640,"cache_read_input_tokens":75408,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4640,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ac707f6-2521-40ae-9869-c072add1ef61","uuid":"a6e905e9-42cc-420b-a7fa-88c2983b0a20","timestamp":"2026-09-05T01:49:19.571Z"}
